{"id": 56, "ts": 1779923589.5625257, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 3, "agent": "agent_2", "message": "I remain convinced that Candidate\u202fC is the strongest overall fit. Building on the points raised by Agents\u202f1 and\u202f3, we now have a concrete stress\u2011resilience indicator:\u202fC \u201ckeeps calm in a crisis,\u201d which directly satisfies the calm\u2011under\u2011pressure criterion that was previously missing for him. Combined with his 100\u202f% reliability, the ability to \u201ccreate a positive atmosphere with his crew,\u201d excellent attention skills, and the proven capacity to \u201cmake correct decisions quickly,\u201d C covers the core safety pillars of reliability, crew cooperation, attention to detail, and decision quality.\nThe remaining concerns \u2013 difficulty communicating ideas, an egocentric attitude, and limited willingness to pursue further education \u2013 are important but less likely to jeopardize crew safety than the pattern of \u201cnasty remarks,\u201d \u201cpretentious\u201d behavior, and \u201cwrong tone\u201d documented for Candidate\u202fB. Moreover, we have no comparable evidence that Candidate\u202fA\u2019s leadership outweighs his reputation as a show\u2011off and his resistance to criticism, nor that Candidate\u202fD\u2019s \u201chot temper\u201d and \u201cknow\u2011it\u2011all\u201d perception won\u2019t impair cockpit teamwork.\nGiven the balance of evidence, I keep my provisional vote for **Candidate\u202fC** and recommend we move toward finalizing that choice, while noting the need for a brief onboarding focus on communication style and continued professional development."}
